                        "entry_id": "87cf227b-0e17-3251-96ef-3250c9e9456a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A species of evergreen myrtle tree, Lophomyrtus bullata."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "New-Zealand",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "26e4a05d-6e45-451a-a177-0e207c13fc1a",
                                "sense_id": "a90a569b-2848-3217-9236-a178f9858a0e",
                                "text": "The ramarama is the largest and most attractive of the New Zealand myrtles: it varies from a dwarf shrub to a small tree 30ft. in height, but is easily distinguished from all other indigenous plants by its reddish-brown leaves, the spaces between the veins of which are tumid or inflated, presenting a singular appearance, as if blistered."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2d76796f-ce24-4cab-8ced-b2b3cd30d513",
                                "sense_id": "a90a569b-2848-3217-9236-a178f9858a0e",
                                "text": "L. bullata (ramarama) is a beautiful shrub or small tree growing to about 5m."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "acd165b0-67d0-4f90-8592-29d4f05bbe95",
                                "sense_id": "a90a569b-2848-3217-9236-a178f9858a0e",
                                "text": "He mentioned a ramarama tree - this, he explained, was not the ramarama, or pepper-tree, of Murihiku, but was a tree like the rohutu, bearing black berries like the konini (fuchsia)."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "31b94cba-d577-40f0-a886-d326f666a3e8",
                                "sense_id": "a90a569b-2848-3217-9236-a178f9858a0e",
                                "text": "When John-Paul Oliver reported his myrtle rust-infected ramarama tree to authorities, they sent him plastics bags and a wool fadge and told him to remove it himself."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
